  "title": {
    "en": "Auks and divers"
  },
  "subtitle": {
    "en": "Guillemot, Razorbill, Puffin, Black Guillemot, Little Auk and the three divers"
  },
  "intro": {
    "en": "Auks and divers are the diving birds of the sea. Auks (guillemots, Razorbills, Puffins) fly low with a fast whirr of wings, and breed on cliffs in summer. In winter they are at sea, and you often see them in winter plumage with white faces. Divers (loons) breed on lakes in the far north and spend the winter at sea.\n\nMost auks pass any sea-watch point from July to October as they leave the colonies, and in winter large numbers sit offshore. The Little Auk is a bird of north Atlantic winters: the species is nowhere near as common as Guillemots, but gales in November can push thousands past the east coast.\n\nThe three divers are the real test. Red-throated is slim with an up-tilted bill; Black-throated is mid-sized, with a straight bill and a neat flank patch; Great Northern is huge, with a thick bill and a dark head."
  },
  "words": [
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "flank patch"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A white patch on the rear flank, near the water line. Black-throated Diver has a neat one that shows when it swims."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "up-tilted"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A bill pointing slightly upwards, as in the Red-throated Diver."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "collar"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A band round the neck. A Great Northern Diver in winter has a pale half-collar."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "winter plumage"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The plain plumage seen outside the breeding season. Auks have white faces and divers a white throat in winter."
      }
    }
  ],
